
Case Study: Troubleshooting Layer 2 Loop & Broadcast Storm

## 📋 Problem Report
- **User Report:** Finance department experiencing severe network latency and intermittent outages.
- **Physical Symptom:** Switch port LEDs flickering rapidly (Broadcast Storm indication).

---

## 🔍 Step 1: Symptom Verification
Checked the Core Switch CPU usage to confirm the impact on the control plane.

```bash
SW-Core# show processes cpu sorted
CPU utilization for five seconds: 98%/1%; one minute: 95%; five minutes: 90%
🛰️ Step 2: Identifying the Source
Checking STP statistics to find the unstable port.

Bash
SW-Core# show spanning-tree detail | include from|occurrence
Number of topology changes 148 last change occurred 00:00:05 ago from GigabitEthernet0/24
Key Finding: The topology is flapping every few seconds, and the changes are originating from interface G0/24.

💡 Step 3: Root Cause Analysis (RCA)
Investigation: Found an unauthorized unmanaged desktop switch connected to port G0/24.

Root Cause: A user created a physical loop on the unmanaged switch (plugged both ends of a cable into it). Since it doesn't run STP, it forwarded the loop back to the Core Switch.

🛠️ Step 4: Resolution & Prevention
Immediate Mitigation
Code snippet
SW-Core(config)# interface g0/24
SW-Core(config-if)# shutdown
Proactive Hardening
Implemented BPDU Guard and PortFast to prevent future incidents.

Code snippet
SW-Core(config-if)# spanning-tree portfast
SW-Core(config-if)# spanning-tree bpduguard enable
SW-Core(config-if)# no shutdown
✅ Step 5: Final Verification
The network stabilized immediately. If the loop is reconnected, the switch now logs:
%PM-4-ERR_DISABLE: bpduguard error detected on Gi0/24, putting Gi0/24 in error-disable state

🧠 Key Takeaways
Visibility: Always monitor CPU and STP topology changes during slowness.

Security: Never leave access ports "naked"; always enable BPDU Guard on ports facing end-users.
